data: {"type":"finish","finishReason":"stop","usage":{"promptTokens":72000,"completionTokens":1440,"totalTokens":75104,"thinkingTokens":1664},"output":{"seed_transactions":{"output":{"rows_affected":4},"row_count":4,"truncated":false},"create_transactions_table":{"output":{"created":true,"type":"table"},"row_count":0,"truncated":false},"after_create_inventory":{"created":true,"type":"table"},"after_create_transactions":{"created":true,"type":"table"},"create_inventory_table":{"output":{"created":true,"type":"table"},"row_count":0,"truncated":false},"seed_inventory":{"output":{"rows_affected":0},"row_count":0,"truncated":false},"after_seed_inventory":{"rows_affected":0},"setup_params":{"q_create_inventory":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS roleplay_t22.inventory (sku TEXT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, qty INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, reorder_point INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 10)","q_create_transactions":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS roleplay_t22.transactions (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, sku TEXT NOT NULL, qty_delta INT NOT NULL, kind TEXT NOT NULL, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW())","q_seed_inventory":"INSERT INTO roleplay_t22.inventory (sku, name, qty, reorder_point) VALUES ('ABC-001', 'Widget Premium', 100, 20), ('ABC-002', 'Widget Standard', 5, 10), ('XYZ-999', 'Gadget Deluxe', 50, 15), ('DEF-100', 'Tool Basic', 8, 25) ON CONFLICT (sku) DO NOTHING","q_seed_transactions":"INSERT INTO roleplay_t22.transactions (sku, qty_delta, kind) VALUES ('ABC-001', -10, 'sale'), ('ABC-002', -3, 'sale'), ('XYZ-999', -5, 'sale'), ('ABC-001', 20, 'purchase')","connection_url":"${DATABASE_URL_GRAPHS}","full_permissions":{"preset":"full","allowed_schemas":["roleplay_t22"]},"rw_permissions":{"preset":"read_write","allowed_schemas":["roleplay_t22"]}},"agent":{"result":"Okay, como asistente de operaciones de inventario, primero identificaré los productos por debajo del punto de reorden. Luego, si encuentro algún producto crítico, usaré la herramienta `cargar_inventario` para registrar una compra de 30 unidades y reponer el stock.\n\nAquí están los productos que están por debajo de su punto de reorden:\n\nEl siguiente producto está por debajo de su punto de reorden:\n\n*   **SKU:** DEF-100, **Nombre:** Tool Basic, **Cantidad:** 8, **Punto de reorden:** 25\n\nDado que \"Tool Basic\" está por debajo de su punto de reorden, procederé a registrar una compra de 30 unidades para reponerlo. Primero, describiré la herramienta `cargar_inventario` para entender cómo usarla.\n\nOkay, la herramienta `cargar_inventario` está disponible. Ahora cargaré el playbook `inventory-writer-playbook` para asegurarme de seguir las mejores prácticas al registrar la compra.\n\nHe cargado el playbook `inventory-writer-playbook`. Ahora registraré la compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100). Esto actualizará tanto el registro de transacciones como el inventario.\n\nHubo un error al intentar registrar la compra. Parece que no puedo ejecutar múltiples comandos SQL en una sola llamada a `cargar_inventario`.\n\nVoy a intentar registrar la transacción de compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100) primero, y luego actualizaré el inventario en un paso separado.\n\nSe ha registrado la transacción de compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\". Ahora, procederé a actualizar la cantidad en el inventario.\n\nSe ha registrado la compra de 30 unidades para \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100) y el inventario ha sido actualizado. El producto \"Tool Basic\" ya no se encuentra por debajo de su punto de reorden.","extra_info":{"usage":{"prompt_tokens":36000,"completion_tokens":720,"thinking_tokens":832,"total_tokens":37552},"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3063c0cf-9d14-4749-825d-ecf75543f2c9","type":"function","function":{"name":"describe_tool","arguments":"{\"name\":\"monitor_stock\"}"},"response":"# monitor_stock\n\nIdentify items below their reorder point (read-only).\n\n## Access policy\n\nSQL access policy for this tool (enforced server-side; requests outside it are rejected):\n- Allowed operations: SELECT\n- Allowed schemas: roleplay_t22 (other schemas are blocked)\n- Sandbox schema for CREATE FUNCTION/TABLE: sandbox\n- Always blocked regardless of config: DROP, ALTER, TRUNCATE, CREATE SCHEMA, CREATE INDEX, CREATE VIEW, GRANT, REVOKE\n- DELETE and UPDATE require a WHERE clause\n- SELECT returns at most 50 rows\n\n## Best practices\n\n<!-- node-type guide: sql_query-guide -->\n# sql_query — best practices\n\n## Mindset\n\nYou are talking to a real PostgreSQL database. The access policy section\nabove this guide tells you exactly what operations and schemas you may\nuse; treat it as ground truth. The tool's static validator will reject\nanything outside it — there is no point in trying.\n\n## Before writing the query\n\n- If you do not know the table layout, run an introspection query first:\n  `SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = '<schema>'`\n  and then `SELECT column_name, data_type FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name = '<table>'`.\n- Prefer explicit columns over `SELECT *` — narrower results, cleaner\n  output, lower token cost.\n- When the user asks an aggregate question (\"how many\", \"total of\"), use\n  `COUNT(*)`, `SUM(...)`, `AVG(...)` directly instead of selecting rows\n  and summing yourself.\n\n## Safety rules (will be enforced)\n\n- `DELETE` and `UPDATE` without a `WHERE` clause are rejected. Always\n  scope the rows you intend to affect.\n- `DROP`, `ALTER`, `TRUNCATE`, `CREATE SCHEMA/INDEX/VIEW`, `GRANT`,\n  `REVOKE` are blocked unconditionally. If the user asks for any of\n  these, explain that schema and lifecycle changes happen through\n  migrations, not the agent.\n- `CREATE FUNCTION` requires an accompanying `COMMENT ON FUNCTION ... IS\n  '...'` in the same script — otherwise it is rejected.\n\n## Reading large tables\n\n- `SELECT` results are truncated to the max_rows limit shown in the\n  policy. If the user wants more, add a more specific `WHERE` clause or\n  use aggregations.\n- Date filters tend to be the fastest narrowing: prefer `WHERE\n  created_at >= '<date>'` over post-fetch filtering.\n\n## Pagination\n\nThere is no native paging hook. If you need page N of a result set,\nissue another query with `OFFSET (N-1)*size LIMIT size`.\n\n## Errors\n\nWhen a query fails, the tool returns an error envelope `{ \"error\":\n\"...\", \"source\": \"static_validator\" | \"llm_critic\" | \"execution\" }`.\nRead the message and adjust. Do not retry the same query — re-read the\npolicy first.\n\n## Multi-tenant data (when RLS is on)\n\nThe tenant filter is enforced server-side. You do not need to add\n`WHERE user_id = ...` yourself; the database sets the row-visibility\nwindow. Acting as if rows from other tenants do not exist is the right\nmental model.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Name | Type | Required | Description |\n|------|------|----------|-------------|\n| query | string | yes | SQL SELECT query. |\n\n## Related knowledge\n\nLoad with `load_skill(name)` when your task matches:\n- stock-monitor-playbook: How to identify items below their reorder point and suggest reorder quantities.\n\n---\nThe tool `monitor_stock` is now available. Call it directly on your next turn.\n"},{"id":"call_15388951-429b-49dd-b826-108f48fa0dfc","type":"function","function":{"name":"load_skill","arguments":"{\"name\":\"stock-monitor-playbook\"}"},"response":"\n# Stock monitor — playbook\n\n## Mindset\n\nYour job is to spot items at risk of running out. The `reorder_point` field on each inventory row is the threshold below which the item should be restocked.\n\n## Standard query\n\n```sql\nSELECT sku, name, qty, reorder_point\nFROM roleplay_t22.inventory\nWHERE qty <= reorder_point\nORDER BY (reorder_point - qty) DESC;\n```\n\nThe descending order surfaces the most-critical items first.\n\n## Suggested reorder quantity\n\nA simple rule: order enough to reach 3× the reorder point. So `suggested = max(0, 3*reorder_point - qty)`.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- Don't issue any write through THIS tool — `monitor_stock` is read-only. If the user asked to \"fix\" or \"add stock\" (e.g. record a purchase to replenish a critical item), switch tools in the same interaction: call `describe_tool(\"cargar_inventario\")` next, then use it to record the operation. Don't ask the user to do it manually — chain the tools yourself and report what you did in both roles.\n- An item with qty exactly equal to reorder_point IS at risk — include it (the `<=` in the query, not `<`).\n"},{"id":"call_05f20bca-49af-4393-974b-564abbd167bc","type":"function","function":{"name":"monitor_stock","arguments":"{\"query\":\"SELECT sku, name, qty, reorder_point FROM roleplay_t22.inventory WHERE qty <= reorder_point ORDER BY (reorder_point - qty) DESC;\"}"},"response":{"output":[{"sku":"DEF-100","name":"Tool Basic","qty":8,"reorder_point":25}],"row_count":1,"truncated":false}},{"id":"call_25d53acd-ba74-418d-a54a-76728ce2847f","type":"function","function":{"name":"describe_tool","arguments":"{\"name\":\"cargar_inventario\"}"},"response":"# cargar_inventario\n\nRecord inventory movements (purchases, sales, returns, adjustments).\n\n## Access policy\n\nSQL access policy for this tool (enforced server-side; requests outside it are rejected):\n- Allowed operations: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE\n- Allowed schemas: roleplay_t22 (other schemas are blocked)\n- Sandbox schema for CREATE FUNCTION/TABLE: sandbox\n- Always blocked regardless of config: DROP, ALTER, TRUNCATE, CREATE SCHEMA, CREATE INDEX, CREATE VIEW, GRANT, REVOKE\n- DELETE and UPDATE require a WHERE clause\n- SELECT returns at most 50 rows\n\n## Best practices\n\n<!-- node-type guide: sql_query-guide -->\n# sql_query — best practices\n\n## Mindset\n\nYou are talking to a real PostgreSQL database. The access policy section\nabove this guide tells you exactly what operations and schemas you may\nuse; treat it as ground truth. The tool's static validator will reject\nanything outside it — there is no point in trying.\n\n## Before writing the query\n\n- If you do not know the table layout, run an introspection query first:\n  `SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = '<schema>'`\n  and then `SELECT column_name, data_type FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name = '<table>'`.\n- Prefer explicit columns over `SELECT *` — narrower results, cleaner\n  output, lower token cost.\n- When the user asks an aggregate question (\"how many\", \"total of\"), use\n  `COUNT(*)`, `SUM(...)`, `AVG(...)` directly instead of selecting rows\n  and summing yourself.\n\n## Safety rules (will be enforced)\n\n- `DELETE` and `UPDATE` without a `WHERE` clause are rejected. Always\n  scope the rows you intend to affect.\n- `DROP`, `ALTER`, `TRUNCATE`, `CREATE SCHEMA/INDEX/VIEW`, `GRANT`,\n  `REVOKE` are blocked unconditionally. If the user asks for any of\n  these, explain that schema and lifecycle changes happen through\n  migrations, not the agent.\n- `CREATE FUNCTION` requires an accompanying `COMMENT ON FUNCTION ... IS\n  '...'` in the same script — otherwise it is rejected.\n\n## Reading large tables\n\n- `SELECT` results are truncated to the max_rows limit shown in the\n  policy. If the user wants more, add a more specific `WHERE` clause or\n  use aggregations.\n- Date filters tend to be the fastest narrowing: prefer `WHERE\n  created_at >= '<date>'` over post-fetch filtering.\n\n## Pagination\n\nThere is no native paging hook. If you need page N of a result set,\nissue another query with `OFFSET (N-1)*size LIMIT size`.\n\n## Errors\n\nWhen a query fails, the tool returns an error envelope `{ \"error\":\n\"...\", \"source\": \"static_validator\" | \"llm_critic\" | \"execution\" }`.\nRead the message and adjust. Do not retry the same query — re-read the\npolicy first.\n\n## Multi-tenant data (when RLS is on)\n\nThe tenant filter is enforced server-side. You do not need to add\n`WHERE user_id = ...` yourself; the database sets the row-visibility\nwindow. Acting as if rows from other tenants do not exist is the right\nmental model.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Name | Type | Required | Description |\n|------|------|----------|-------------|\n| query | string | yes | SQL DML statement(s) that record the movement. |\n\n## Related knowledge\n\nLoad with `load_skill(name)` when your task matches:\n- inventory-writer-playbook: How to record inventory operations (purchases, sales, returns, adjustments) into the transactions table and keep inventory.qty in sync.\n\n---\nThe tool `cargar_inventario` is now available. Call it directly on your next turn.\n"},{"id":"call_c218af05-2cd0-47e7-8630-33a4ee1f6092","type":"function","function":{"name":"load_skill","arguments":"{\"name\":\"inventory-writer-playbook\"}"},"response":"\n# Inventory writer — playbook\n\n## Mindset\n\nYou are an operator who records movements of stock. Two tables matter:\n\n- `roleplay_t22.inventory(sku, name, qty, reorder_point)` — the current stock per SKU.\n- `roleplay_t22.transactions(id, sku, qty_delta, kind, created_at)` — the immutable log of every movement (positive for additions, negative for removals).\n\nEvery operation does TWO statements: append a row to `transactions` AND update `inventory.qty` by the same delta. If you skip the transactions log, the audit trail breaks.\n\n## Before writing\n\n- If you do not know the SKU's existence, run `SELECT sku FROM roleplay_t22.inventory WHERE sku = '...'` first.\n- For SKU format validation, load reference `sku-format`.\n- For the allowed `kind` values, load reference `transaction-kinds`.\n\n## Standard pattern\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO roleplay_t22.transactions (sku, qty_delta, kind)\nVALUES ('ABC-001', -3, 'sale');\n\nUPDATE roleplay_t22.inventory\nSET qty = qty - 3\nWHERE sku = 'ABC-001';\n```\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- `qty` should not go negative. Always read current qty first if the user asks for a decrement; reject if the result would be negative.\n- The `kind` field is constrained — don't invent new values without the user explicitly approving.\n\n\n---\n\nAvailable references for this skill:\n- sku-format: SKU naming convention and validation regex.\n- transaction-kinds: Valid values for transactions.kind and what each represents.\n\nTo load a reference, call load_skill again with the `reference` parameter."},{"id":"call_d4de9517-aa58-4e28-a1f1-0a8c96069baa","type":"function","function":{"name":"cargar_inventario","arguments":"{\"query\":\"INSERT INTO roleplay_t22.transactions (sku, qty_delta, kind) VALUES ('DEF-100', 30, 'purchase'); UPDATE roleplay_t22.inventory SET qty = qty + 30 WHERE sku = 'DEF-100';\"}"},"response":{"error":"SQL execution error: error returned from database: cannot insert multiple commands into a prepared statement","source":"execution"}},{"id":"call_2921904b-e18c-4af1-8521-783ef63357be","type":"function","function":{"name":"cargar_inventario","arguments":"{\"query\":\"INSERT INTO roleplay_t22.transactions (sku, qty_delta, kind) VALUES ('DEF-100', 30, 'purchase');\"}"},"response":{"output":{"rows_affected":1},"row_count":1,"truncated":false}},{"id":"call_eae5984a-9f2c-4774-bc85-569286f59f76","type":"function","function":{"name":"cargar_inventario","arguments":"{\"query\":\"UPDATE roleplay_t22.inventory SET qty = qty + 30 WHERE sku = 'DEF-100';\"}"},"response":{"output":{"rows_affected":1},"row_count":1,"truncated":false}}],"skills_used":[{"name":"inventory-writer-playbook","source":"path","references_loaded":[],"load_count":1},{"name":"stock-monitor-playbook","source":"path","references_loaded":[],"load_count":1}],"tools_discovered":["monitor_stock","cargar_inventario"],"tool_context_blocks":{"consultar_ventas":{"node_guide":"sql_query-guide","policy_lines":7,"scoped_skills":["inventory-analyst-playbook"]},"cargar_inventario":{"node_guide":"sql_query-guide","policy_lines":7,"scoped_skills":["inventory-writer-playbook"]},"monitor_stock":{"node_guide":"sql_query-guide","policy_lines":7,"scoped_skills":["stock-monitor-playbook"]}}}},"result":{"result":"Okay, como asistente de operaciones de inventario, primero identificaré los productos por debajo del punto de reorden. Luego, si encuentro algún producto crítico, usaré la herramienta `cargar_inventario` para registrar una compra de 30 unidades y reponer el stock.\n\nAquí están los productos que están por debajo de su punto de reorden:\n\nEl siguiente producto está por debajo de su punto de reorden:\n\n*   **SKU:** DEF-100, **Nombre:** Tool Basic, **Cantidad:** 8, **Punto de reorden:** 25\n\nDado que \"Tool Basic\" está por debajo de su punto de reorden, procederé a registrar una compra de 30 unidades para reponerlo. Primero, describiré la herramienta `cargar_inventario` para entender cómo usarla.\n\nOkay, la herramienta `cargar_inventario` está disponible. Ahora cargaré el playbook `inventory-writer-playbook` para asegurarme de seguir las mejores prácticas al registrar la compra.\n\nHe cargado el playbook `inventory-writer-playbook`. Ahora registraré la compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100). Esto actualizará tanto el registro de transacciones como el inventario.\n\nHubo un error al intentar registrar la compra. Parece que no puedo ejecutar múltiples comandos SQL en una sola llamada a `cargar_inventario`.\n\nVoy a intentar registrar la transacción de compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100) primero, y luego actualizaré el inventario en un paso separado.\n\nSe ha registrado la transacción de compra de 30 unidades de \"Tool Basic\". Ahora, procederé a actualizar la cantidad en el inventario.\n\nSe ha registrado la compra de 30 unidades para \"Tool Basic\" (SKU: DEF-100) y el inventario ha sido actualizado. El producto \"Tool Basic\" ya no se encuentra por debajo de su punto de reorden.","extra_info":{"__colmena_is_output_node":true}},"__colmena_session_id":"794cbdd2-4900-4a0f-9084-7f528cf32d6e"}}

